    "notes": "Test piece for the six-motif / two-counterpoint experiment. Simpler and shorter than Xanadu Voyage on purpose: 6 sections, 64 bars, ~2 minutes at 128bpm, D mixolydian (I-VII-IV rock vamp, no chromatic bass tricks). IMPORTANT lesson from first render: section-level 'motif' (top-level key) is a no-op for melody -- confirmed by the engine's own lint. The wired path is melody-as-dict (VoiceModel shape) with 'motif' embedded inside it, which is what every section below actually uses. transform_pool:[] on chorus_a/chorus_b's motifs verified against melody.py's generate_develop: empty pool -> transform_name resolves to None -> untransformed base intervals every retiling -- genuine riff repetition, not development. Bass in both chorus halves uses bass_style:'motif' so bass and lead state the same riff in unison (bass.py's style_motif threads the section's active_motif_def through). Two counterpoint voices (above + below) run from the verse through the bridge. harmony_rhythm:'sustain' added everywhere melody uses rhythm:'motif', after the first render showed 'motif' rhythm on a section cascades to harmony as 'free' (NOT 'motif' -- that's deliberate, see schemas.py), producing 7-12x more chord events than melody notes and burying the tune; 'sustain' holds the harmonic span instead of re-onsetting constantly. Lead velocities raised for headroom over bass/harmony defaults (70/65). Suggested instruments, following the recent 'simple synths reveal detail better' conclusion: Juno-6V for bass (clean DCO, no drift to muddy the riff unison), Jup-8V or Mini V for lead, Mellotron flutes or a second simple pad for the two counterpoint voices kept in contrasting registers so they stay separable by ear.",
